    const string inputHelp = @"
Automatic downloads and inputs
  Sockseek can download automatically and in bulk. You give it an input, such as a search string, a
  playlist link or a CSV file, and for each item it searches, picks the best match using your
  ranking and filtering conditions, and downloads it.
  - In the WebUI, open Searches → New Job.
  - In the CLI, every download works this way, unless you add -t/--interactive to pick album folders
    yourself.
  The rest of this page lists the input types. The CLI usually detects the type of an input; to set
  it, use --input-type.

  CSV file
    A path to a local CSV file listing songs or albums.
    In the CSV file, rows with a track title are song downloads, and rows without a title are
    treated as album downloads. Only a title or album column is required in the CSV file (extra info
    may improve search results). If the columns have common names (like 'Artist', 'Title', 'Album',
    'Length', etc) then it's not required to manually specify them, otherwise you must provide at
    least --title-col or --album-col.
    See CSV options for all options.

  YouTube
    A YouTube playlist URL.
    The default method to retrieve playlists might not reliably return all videos. If you're having
    issues, you can use the official API by providing a key with --youtube-key. A key can be
    obtained at https://console.cloud.google.com (create a new project, click 'Enable API' and
    search for 'YouTube Data', then follow the prompts).
    See YouTube options for all options.

  Spotify
    Spotify inputs support:
    - Public and private playlist URLs
    - Album URLs
    - Liked songs, with sockseek spotify-likes
    - Liked albums, with sockseek spotify-albums
    Spotify API access requires your own Spotify developer application for all Spotify inputs,
    including public playlists. Spotify also requires the owner of that application to have an
    active Spotify Premium subscription. If you do not have Premium, you can export the Spotify
    playlist with a Spotify-to-CSV converter and pass the CSV file to Sockseek instead.
    See Spotify options for all options.

    Using Credentials
      Create a Spotify application at https://developer.spotify.com/dashboard/applications with a
      redirect URL http://127.0.0.1:48721/callback. The Spotify account that owns the application
      must have an active Premium subscription. Obtain an application ID and secret from the created
      application dashboard.
      For public playlists and albums, pass the application credentials:

      sockseek ""https://open.spotify.com/playlist/id"" --spotify-id 123456 --spotify-secret 123456

      For private playlists, liked songs, liked albums, or --remove-from-source, start Sockseek with
      the obtained credentials and an action that requires auth to trigger the Spotify app login
      flow:

      sockseek spotify-likes --spotify-id 123456 --spotify-secret 123456 -n 1 --print jobs

      Sockseek will try to open a browser automatically but will fall back to logging the login flow
      URL to output. After login flow is complete it will output a token and refresh token and
      finish running the current command.
      To skip requiring login flow every time Sockseek is used the token and refresh token can be
      provided (hint: store this info in the config file to make commands less verbose):

      sockseek spotify-likes --spotify-id 123456 --spotify-secret 123456 --spotify-refresh 123456 --spotify-token 123456 -n 1 --pt

      spotify-token access is only valid for 1 hour. spotify-refresh will enable Sockseek to renew
      access every time it is run (and can be used without including spotify-token)

  Bandcamp
    Bandcamp inputs support:
    - Track URLs
    - Album URLs
    - Artist URLs, to download all albums by the artist.
    - Wishlist URLs, to download all albums on the wishlist.
    Extraction might fail due to Cloudflare; download the HTML to a local file and point Sockseek to
    it using --from-html in case of issues.

  MusicBrainz
    MusicBrainz inputs support:
    - /release/... URLs
    - /release-group/... URLs, where it picks the most common version of the album.
    - /collection/... URLs, to download a list of albums.

  SoundCloud
    SoundCloud inputs support:
    - Track URLs
    - Public and private playlist URLs
    Requires a SoundCloud OAuth token, passed with --soundcloud-token, also for public items.
    When a track has no publisher artist, the artist is taken from titles like ""Artist - Title"",
    falling back to the uploader's name.

    Getting the OAuth token
      1. Log in to https://soundcloud.com in your browser.
      2. Open the developer tools (F12) and go to the Storage tab (Application in Chrome).
      3. Under Cookies > https://soundcloud.com, copy the value of oauth_token.
      Pass it with --soundcloud-token, or put it in your config file:

      soundcloud-token = <your-token>

  Soulseek Link
    A direct path starting with slsk://. By default, a path ending in / is an ordinary folder
    download and a path without it is an ordinary exact-file download. Use --song or --album to
    request music-specific handling. --song rejects directory links; --album treats the supplied
    remote path as the selected album directory, whether or not it has a trailing slash.

  Search string
    A string input is a name of the track, album, or artist to search for. They come in these forms:
    1. Arbitrary search queries (like what you would type in the Soulseek search bar), e.g. sockseek
       ""blah blah"".
    This is equivalent to sockseek ""album=blah blah"" by default, and to sockseek ""title=blah
    blah"" when using -s/--song mode.
    2. An artist-album or artist-song combination, separated by a hyphen with spaces -:
    - sockseek ""Artist - Album"" downloads an album
    - sockseek ""Artist - Song"" -s downloads a song
    It's always better to split the artist from song/album like this when you want to provide an
    artist due to ranking and filtering.
    This is equivalent to sockseek ""artist=Artist, album=Album"" in the first case, and to sockseek
    ""artist=Artist, title=Song"" in the second case.
    3. A comma-separated list of properties, like title=Song Name, artist=Artist Name, length=215.
       With an album field (and no title) it's treated as an album download. With a title field it's
       treated as a song download (even if it includes an album field).
    Accepted fields are:
    - Song title title
    - Artist name artist
    - Album name album
    - Song length (in seconds)
    Notes:
    - You can combine the second and third form, e.g. Artist - Song, length=123 is the same as
      artist=Artist, title=Song, length=123 in --song mode.
    - You can use ""artist=Artist, title=Song"" --album if you want title= to act as an album search
      hint, i.e. you want to search for an album by the name of one of its tracks.

  List file
    A path to a local text file listing items of any supported input type. List inputs must be
    manually activated with --input-type=list.
    Each line has an input followed by optional required and preferred conditions. Separate the
    fields with at least two spaces or a tab.

    Any input type       conditions (optional)      pref. conditions (optional)

    Here is an example list file:

    Artist - Album    strict-album=true; album-track-count>=13     br >= 320

    # Use s: for one song, or --song to treat all unprefixed string entries as songs.
    s:Artist - Song   strict-title=true

    # Any other input type is also accepted:
    path/to/tracks.csv
    https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=blah

    # Quote a field to preserve repeated spaces within it:
    s:""Artist - Song  (Live)""   ""strict-title=true; format=flac""

    The conditions are added on top of the configured conditions and can be omitted. Lines starting
    with # are treated as comments and ignored.";

    const string downloadModesHelp = @"
Download modes
  There are five download modes: Song, Album, Song Groups, Album Groups, and Generic. In the GUI,
  you can select which mode to use for the next search using the button in the search bar, and set
  the default using the web-default-search-mode option in the config. For automated downloads,
  sources usually decide for themselves whether they contain songs or albums. In the CLI, string
  inputs are treated as albums by default.

  Song
    Song mode is the default for playlists from streaming platforms and CSV song lists, and
    downloads one file per item. In the CLI, use -s/--song for string/list input that should be
    treated as a song search. A list of songs can be upgraded to album downloads using
    --upgrade-to-album (albums will be deduplicated).
    CLI Example (string input):

    sockseek ""Artist - Song Name"" -s

  Album
    In Album mode, Sockseek downloads whole folders. It is the default for CLI string inputs and
    album sources such as Spotify/Bandcamp album links and CSV album rows. In the CLI, -t to pick
    interactively.
    CLI Example (string input):

    sockseek ""Artist - Album Name"" -t

    See Album options for album-specific options.

  Album Groups
    Album Groups mode is activated when -g/--group is enabled for album inputs. In this mode,
    Sockseek will attempt to group folders returned by Soulseek and download one of each distinct
    album, starting with the one shared by the most peers.
    Folders are grouped by their track count and track lengths, each within --group-length-tol (3
    seconds by default). When lengths can't tell albums apart, as with a single track, titles must
    match too. Within a group, the versions are ranked by your other preferences.
    Note that --min-group-shares is set to 2 by default, meaning that albums shared by only one user
    will be ignored. Album Groups mode can be used to determine the most common version of a
    particular album, or download the most popular (or all) albums by an artist. It's recommended to
    pair it with -t/--interactive in the CLI, which allows you to pick the particular version of the
    album in each group and skip groups you don't want.
    CLI Example:

    sockseek ""artist=Artist Name"" -gt

    See Grouping options for group options.

  Song Groups
    Song Groups mode is enabled with --group --song (or -gs for short). Sockseek will attempt to
    group files returned by Soulseek and download one of each distinct song, starting with the one
    shared by the most peers.
    Files are grouped by the artist and title read from each file name, with lengths within
    --group-length-tol (3 seconds by default).
    As in Album Groups mode, --min-group-shares is set to 2 by default, meaning that songs shared by
    only one user will be ignored. This mode is useful for downloading all songs by an artist
    available on Soulseek.
    CLI Example:

    sockseek ""artist=Artist Name"" -gs

    See Grouping options for group options.

  Generic Files
    This mode is only available in the GUI and behaves much like the usual search in clients like
    SoulseekQt and Nicotine+. Unlike album and song modes, it does not set any default filters for
    music, and does no special ranking of search results beyond the usual sorting mode (like by
    speed or by file size) that you chose. This mode is useful when you're looking for non-music
    downloads, like PDFs or videos.";

    const string rankingAndFilteringHelp = @"
Ranking and filtering
  Sockseek supports filter as well as ranking configuration.
  A required condition filters files from search results. For example, --format flac will reject
  non-flac files entirely.
  Ranking (or ""preferred"") conditions start with the pref- prefix and change how results are
  ranked; they never filter anything out. Ranking affects how results are sorted when using
  ""Relevance"" sorting in the GUI, and how results are chosen in automated downloads. For example,
  in an automated download,--pref-format flac means Sockseek will prefer flac when available, but
  will still download mp3 if no flacs are found.

  Filtering
    The only default required conditions are for the files to be audio:

    format = mp3,flac,ogg,m4a,opus,wav,aac,alac

    This only applies for music-oriented search modes (song mode, album mode, etc); generic file
    searches in the GUI start with no filters at all.

  Ranking
    The preferred conditions determine ranking. The defaults are:

    # Prefer songs whose duration differs from the supplied one
    # by at most 3 seconds.
    pref-length-tol = 3

    # Prefer files whose paths contain the exact title and album name,
    # surrounded by boundary characters like punctuation (see below).
    # Disabling these is usually not recommended.
    pref-strict-title = true
    pref-strict-album = true

    # Prefer results that aren't locked. This is only relevant for
    # interactive searches in the WebUI. Automated downloads always
    # ignore locked items.
    pref-exclude-locked = true

    When results differ in several preferences, they're ranked in this order:
    1. Not from a downranked user (pref-banned-users)
    2. Strict title, then strict album name, then strict artist name
    3. For songs, Length within tolerance
    4. From a preferred user (pref-allowed-users)
    5. Format, then bitrate, sample rate and bit depth
    6. Not locked
    For example, with pref-strict-title enabled and pref-format=flac, an MP3 file that contains the
    song name still ranks above a FLAC file that doesn't.
    WARNING
    The order of formats in a list has no effect. Both --pref-format mp3,flac and --pref-format
    flac,mp3 give MP3 and FLAC equal preference.
    For debugging, you can run a song search with --print results-full to reveal the sorting logic.

  Albums
    For album downloads, conditions like format, bitrate and sample rate operate based on coverage:
    A folder with 9 FLAC files and 1 MP3 is preferred over a mostly-MP3 folder, and the selected
    folder is still downloaded as a whole. Use --strict-album-quality to require every audio file in
    the folder to satisfy the conditions.

  Setting conditions
    See the options reference for all filtering and ranking options.
    Conditions can also be supplied as a semicolon-delimited string with --cond and --pref, e.g.:

    --cond ""br>=320; format=mp3,ogg; sr<96000; album-track-count>=8""

    File Search in the UI starts with no file conditions. To set defaults for a particular search
    mode, use an auto-profile with an input-type or a download-mode condition. See search defaults.

  Unknown metadata
    Some info may be unavailable depending on the client used by the peer (like the bitrate). This
    is why if you set a --min-bitrate, Sockseek will still accept any file with unknown bitrate as a
    precaution.
    To reject all files where one or more of the checked properties is null (unknown), use
    --strict-conditions.
    This flag should be used with care: It's easy to accidentally exclude all files from users with
    certain clients. For example, because the SoulseekQt client does not broadcast the bitrate,
    enabling --strict-conditions and setting a --min-bitrate will effectively exclude all files
    shared by users with the standard client(!).

  Strict-artist, strict-album, strict-title
    These check that the result filepaths contain what you searched for, as a whole name rather than
    part of a longer one:
    - Artist must appear anywhere in the file's path.
    - Album must appear in the path. The file name doesn't count.
    - For songs, Title must appear in the file name (without its extension).
    Each comes as a requirement (strict-title, strict-artist, strict-album) and as a preference
    (pref-strict-title, pref-strict-artist, pref-strict-album). Casing and accents are ignored, and
    _ counts as a space, as do characters that can't appear in file names on some systems, like :
    and ?.
    Artist must be whole words, with a space or punctuation on each side (or the start/end of the
    text). Joe Yamanaka matches Joe Yamanaka - Best, but not Joe Yamanakas.
    Title and album are stricter: only punctuation (such as -, (, ), [, ], ., ,), a folder
    separator, or the start or end of the text may surround them. A space alone is not enough
    (except after a number such as a track number or year). For Song name and Ashita no Joe:
    - 01 Song name.flac, Artist - Song name (Live).flac, Artist - Ashita no Joe [FLAC] and 1980
      Ashita no Joe match.
    - Song name II.flac and Ashita no Joe 2 Soundtrack do not, because II and 2 Soundtrack continue
      the name.
    Some cases are handled specially:
    - Several artists separated by ; match if any one of them appears. Featured artists (feat.) are
      optional.
    - Self-titled songs and albums, where the album has the artist's name, would match every folder
      of that artist. For these, the album folder must contain nothing but the name, apart from
      bracketed text, years and punctuation. Led Zeppelin (Remaster) matches, but Led Zeppelin II
      doesn't. Note that for Sockseek to be able to recognize that your input is self-titled in the
      first place, you must fill in both the artist and album/song inputs when searching.

  Other ranking heuristics
    Sockseek uses other heuristics in addition to the pref-strict-* checks, most of which are not
    worth mentioning. The notable ones:
    - Incomplete albums. Peers sometimes share only some tracks of an album. Sockseek takes the
      track count that most matching folders share as the reference, and ranks folders with fewer
      tracks below complete ones, after matching but before audio quality. This is
      pref-common-track-count, on by default except in manual searches and --interactive.
    - Unrequested versions. For songs, a file marked as a different version in brackets, such as
      (Live), (Remix) or [Instrumental], ranks below the original unless your search includes that
      word.
    - Near matches. Results that contain your search terms loosely rank between strict matches and
      results that don't contain them.
    - Unreliable peers in automated downloads. A peer with 2 failed downloads in a run ranks lower;
      after 3, it's ignored.
    - Availability. Among otherwise equal results, a free upload slot and a faster upload speed come
      first.";

    const string nameFormatHelp = @"
Name format
  Use --name-format to choose how Sockseek names downloaded files and arranges them in folders.
  When --name-format is blank (the default), songs keep their filenames, albums and whole folders
  keep their tree, and a named list or playlist may add an enclosing folder.
  Suppose 01.flac has the artist tag North Yard and the title tag Opening. Sockseek substitutes
  those values wherever you write {artist} and {title}:

  {artist} - {title}

  Saves the file as North Yard - Opening.flac.

  {artist}/{title}

  Saves it as North Yard/Opening.flac, with a folder for the artist.
  These two forms are simple, but they assume every file has the tags. A missing tag is left blank,
  so a file with no artist tag is saved as - Opening.flac. In almost every case you should use the
  form below, which falls back to the original filename.

  {artist( - )title|filename}

  Prefer this form. It tries the part before | first. If both tags are present, it inserts the text
  inside ( - ) between them, giving North Yard - Opening.flac. If either tag is missing, it uses
  filename and keeps 01.flac. Note that you can chain | more than once.

  {albumartist(/)album(/)track(. )title|(missing-tags/)slsk-foldername(/)slsk-filename}

  Organizes tagged tracks by album artist and album. If a required tag is missing, the file goes
  under missing-tags with its remote folder and filename.
  See the variable reference or run sockseek --help variables.";

    const string variablesHelp = @"
Variables
  Variables are shared by name formats and on-complete commands.

  Shared placement variables
    These variables are available for all download jobs.

    peer-username / username        Username of the peer serving the file
    filename / slsk-filename        Remote filename without its extension
    ext                             Final output file extension, including the dot
    relative-path                   Relative directory plus filename, without the extension
    foldername / slsk-foldername    Selected root plus relative directory; for a direct
                                    file, its immediate remote parent folder
    item-name                       Current song, album, file, or folder name
    default-folder                  Enclosing list/playlist folders for the current job
                                    (e.g. Spotify playlist name)
    output-dir / outputdir          Output parent directory (--output)
    type                            Job type (Song, Album, RemoteFile, RemoteDirectory, ..)
    extractor                       Name of the input extractor (Spotify, CSV, ..)
    input                           Original input string
    bindir                          Base application directory
    configdir                       Active config file directory

  Music variables
    These are read from the downloaded file's tags:

    artist                         First artist
    artists                        Artists, joined with '&'
    albumartist                    First album artist
    albumartists                   Album artists, joined with '&'
    title                          Track title
    album                          Album name
    year                           Track year
    track                          Track number
    disc                           Disc number
    length                         Track length (in seconds)

    These are taken from the input source (CSV file data, Spotify, etc):

    sartist / sartists             Source artist
    stitle                         Source track title
    salbum                         Source album name
    slength                        Source track length
    uri / url                      Track URI
    snum                           Source item number (1-indexed, including offset)
    row / line                     Line number (1-indexed, only for CSV or list input)
    artist-maybe-wrong             If the source artist might be incorrect (true/false)";
    
    const string configHelp = @"
Configuration

  Config Location
    Sockseek will look for a file named sockseek.conf in the following locations:
    - ~/.config/sockseek/sockseek.conf (Any platform)
    - %APPDATA%\sockseek\sockseek.conf (Windows only)
    - $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sockseek/sockseek.conf
    - {sockseek executable dir}/sockseek.conf
    Use --config <path> to choose a config file, --config none or --no-config to skip config
    loading.
    The daemon automatically reloads config changes; share changes require a rescan, and some
    settings still require a restart.

  Syntax
    Example config file:

    username = your-username
    password = your-password
    pref-format = flac
    fast-search = true

    Every command-line option is also a config setting: drop the leading -- and write it as name =
    value. For example --pref-format flac becomes pref-format = flac, and a flag such as
    --fast-search becomes fast-search = true. See options for everything you can set, and daemon
    options for the daemon, WebUI and sharing settings.
    Lines starting with # will be treated as comments. Tildes in paths are expanded as the user
    directory (even on Windows). Path settings also support {bindir} for the Sockseek binary
    directory and {configdir} for the directory containing the active config file.

  Configuration profiles
    Sockseek supports configuration profiles:

    [lossless]
    pref-format = flac,wav

    To activate the above profile, use --profile lossless. This will apply all of its options. Your
    manual CLI options still take precedence.
    To list all configured profiles, run --profile help.
    Some settings apply to the whole Sockseek process rather than to one download, for example
    user-description, user-picture, concurrent-jobs and log-file. They take effect only when the
    profile is chosen at startup with --profile. A profile containing them cannot be an auto-profile
    or be selected for a single download in the WebUI or remote CLI.

  Auto-profiles
    Profiles can be activated automatically based on a few simple conditions. For example:

    # never automatically cancel album downloads in interactive mode
    [no-stale]
    profile-cond = interactive && download-mode == ""album""
    max-stale-time = 9999999

    # download to another location for YouTube
    [youtube]
    profile-cond = input-type == ""youtube""
    output-dir = ~/downloads/sockseek-youtube

    # use structural naming for non-music downloads
    [generic-layout]
    profile-cond = download-mode == ""generic""
    name-format = {peer-username}/{foldername}/{filename}

    The following operators are supported for use in profile-cond: &&, ||, ==, !=, !{bool}. The
    following variables are available:

    input-type        ""youtube""|""csv""|""string""|""bandcamp""|""spotify""|""list""|
                      ""musicbrainz""|""soulseek-link""|""manual-search""|
                      ""peer-shares""|""none""
    download-mode     ""song""|""song-groups""|""album""|""album-groups""|""generic""
    album             bool
    group             bool
    interactive       bool
    progress-json     bool
    no-progress       bool

    WebUI search defaults
      Use input-type == ""manual-search"" in an auto-profile to set defaults for the WebUI search
      bar. Add a download-mode condition to tailor the defaults for one search mode.

      [webui-search]
      profile-cond = input-type == ""manual-search""
      search-timeout = 20000
      pref-format = flac

      [webui-album-search]
      profile-cond = input-type == ""manual-search"" && download-mode == ""album""
      name-format = {albumartist(/)album(/)track(. )title|(missing-tags/)slsk-foldername(/)slsk-filename}

  Lists
    Lists can be written on a single line, separated by commas:

    banned-users = Alice,Bob

    Or multiline:

    banned-users =
      - Alice
      - Bob

    Leave the list empty (for example, banned-users = on its own line) to clear inherited items. Use
    = + to append to the current list, including values inherited from another profile. Commas and
    leading plus signs inside an item are literal.

    [weekend]
    allowed-users = +
        - Bob
        - + Friends

  Multiline text
    For a string setting, put | after the equals sign and indent the text below it.

    user-description = |
      Blah
      Blah blah blah

      music!";

    const string onCompleteHelp = @"
On-Complete Actions
  The --on-complete option allows executing commands when a file or folder download finishes.
  Multiple actions can be chained using the + prefix (note the space after +).
  Syntax: --on-complete ""[options] -- command""
  Hint: You can use --mock-files-dir to test your commands (see Testing Options).
  Every on-complete command must include the -- delimiter. Sockseek options go before it; everything
  after it is the command passed to the OS.
  When passing an on-complete action on the command line, quote the whole value so the delimiter is
  part of the --on-complete argument:

  --on-complete ""when=success scope=folder -- notify-send \""Downloaded\"" \""{path}\""""

  Options
    - when=completed - Execute on success or fail, if the item wasn't skipped
    - when=success - Execute only for successful downloads
    - when=failure - Execute for failed or partially successful downloads
    - when=skipped - Execute for skipped jobs
    - when=already-exists - Execute only for already-existing skipped jobs
    - when=not-found-last-time - Execute only for not-found-last-time skipped jobs
    - when=cancelled - Execute only for cancelled jobs
    - when=any - Execute for every terminal outcome
    - scope=file - Run for each file, including files in a folder or album download.
    - scope=folder - Run once for the whole folder or album.
    - Omit scope to run for both. A successful three-file folder triggers a when=success command
      four times.
    - hidden - Hide the command window
    - shell - Use shell execute
    - lock - Serialize this action across jobs
    - update-index - Read stdout only if the command exits with code 0. Use success;new_path to
      record success and the new path, failed to record failure and clear the path, or
      ignored;new_path to update only the path.
    If when= is omitted, it behaves like when=completed. This preserves the usual ""run when work
    completed"" behavior while avoiding commands for already-existing or not-found-last-time skips.
    Chained actions evaluate when= and outcome/path variables against the result of the preceding
    action. For example, if an update-index action changes a failure to success, the next
    when=success action runs and sees the updated path.

  Command-output variables
    See the shared Variables reference, also available with sockseek --help variables. On-complete
    commands additionally provide:

    is-audio            true for an audio file; false otherwise
    path                Absolute downloaded file or directory path
    path-noext          Absolute downloaded path without its final file extension

    terminal-outcome    Succeeded, Failed, Skipped, Cancelled, or PartialSuccess
    skip-reason         AlreadyExists, NotFoundLastTime, Manual, or Filtered;
                        None when the outcome is not a skip or has no specific reason
    failure-reason      None, InvalidSearchString, OutOfDownloadRetries,
                        AllDownloadsFailed, Other, ExtractionFailed, Cancelled,
                        ChildJobsFailed, NoSearchResults, or NoMatchingResults

    exitcode            Previous command's exit code
    stdout              Previous command's stdout
    stderr              Previous command's stderr
    first-exitcode      First command's exit code
    first-stdout        First command's stdout
    first-stderr        First command's stderr

    Sockseek captures stdout/stderr for ordinary on-complete commands, so chained commands can use
    output variables from the previous ones. Commands launched with shell cannot use stdout/stderr.
    For folder actions on album jobs, tag-based variables such as {title}, {artist}, and {album} are
    read from the first audio file in the album.

  Examples
    This auto-profile applies to album downloads. The folder scope sends one Linux desktop
    notification when the whole album succeeds.

    [album-notification]
    profile-cond = album
    on-complete = when=success scope=folder -- notify-send ""Downloaded: {album}"" ""{path}""

    The following examples are for Windows. Search album art with Cover Fetcher:

    on-complete = when=success scope=folder hidden -- cmd /c start """" ""path\to\CoverFetcher.exe"" --from-dir ""{path}""

    Queue downloaded audio files in foobar2000:

    on-complete = when=success scope=file hidden -- cmd /c if {is-audio}==true start """" ""path\to\foobar2000.exe"" /immediate /add ""{path}""

    Convert downloaded audio files to MP3 on Windows (requires ffmpeg):

    # Check if file is audio and not already MP3
    on-complete =   when=success hidden -- cmd /c if ""{is-audio}""==""true"" if /i not ""{ext}""=="".mp3"" if not exist ""{path-noext}.mp3"" echo true

    # Convert to MP3 if check passed
    on-complete = + when=success hidden -- cmd /c if /i ""{stdout}""==""true"" (ffmpeg -i ""{path}"" -q:a 0 ""{path-noext}.mp3"" && echo success)

    # Delete original and update index if conversion succeeded
    on-complete = + when=success hidden update-index -- cmd /c if /i ""{stdout}""==""success"" (del ""{path}"" & echo ""ignored;{path-noext}.mp3"")";

    const string shortcutsHelp = @"
CLI Shortcuts & interactive mode

  CLI Shortcuts
    You can use these at any time while things are searching or downloading:

    c               cancel specific job by id or all jobs
    t               try next candidate for a job id
    i               get detailed info about job by id

  CLI Interactive Prompt Shortcuts
    Interactive mode for albums can be enabled with -t/--interactive. It allows you to pick the
    desired folder or download only specific files from it, rather than automatically downloading
    the best match.
    Key bindings:

    Up/p            previous folder
    Down/n          next folder
    Enter/d         download selected folder
    y               download folder and disable interactive mode
    r               retrieve all files in the folder
    s/q/Esc         skip current album
    Q/S             skip current and all remaining new album prompts
    h               print this help text

    d:1,2,3         download specific files
    d:start-end     download a range of files
    f               filter folders containing files matching query
    cd ..           load parent folder
    cd subdir       go to subfolder";

    const string notesAndTipsHelp = @"
Troubleshooting and tips

  Rate limits
    The Soulseek server bans users for 30 minutes if too many searches are performed within a short
    timespan. Sockseek has a search limiter which can be adjusted with --searches-per-time and
    --searches-renew-time (when the limit is reached, the status of the downloads will be
    'Waiting'). By default it is configured to allow up to 34 searches every 220 seconds.

  Why are some of my automated downloads wrong or missing?

    It downloaded a wrong song or album!
      By default, Sockseek doesn't do any filtering on the results returned by Soulseek, erring on
      the side of downloading incorrect files rather than missing files entirely. If the correct
      result exists among the things returned by the network, it will usually be chosen. But if
      Soulseek doesn't have it, Sockseek might download something incorrect. If you're bothered by
      this, you can make matching more strict as shown below, at the cost of increased chance of
      missing correct items in other cases.
      For song downloads, you can:
      - Set the length tolerance --length-tol to something like 3, in order to ignore all files
        whose length differs from the supplied length by more than 3 seconds. This is useful only
        when your source actually provides length metadata, such as in the case of playlists from
        streaming services or a CSV file with a track duration column. Note that lengths from
        streaming services can differ from the actual files on Soulseek (like from a CD rip).
      - Add filters for the title and artist:

        sockseek ""https://spotify.com/playlist/blah"" --strict-title --strict-artist

        These require that the file path contains the song title and artist name (case-insensitive).
        Be careful with spelling differences and weird names.
      For album downloads, the cleanest filter is usually the expected track count:

      sockseek ""Artist - Album"" --album-track-count 10

      Use an inequality like 10+ when an expanded edition is acceptable. You can also add
      --strict-album to require the album name in the folder path, or --strict-artist as in the song
      case.

    It says no results for a song or album!
      Sockseek passes your input (i.e. artist + song name in case of songs, and artist + album name
      in case of albums) directly to Soulseek. It doesn't do any hard filtering by default, which
      means that no results almost always implies that your input itself has no matches on Soulseek.
      Some things to try:
      - Clean up your inputs when they contain uncommon names or artist spellings, for example, with
        options like --regex and --remove-ft. See also the next section.
      - Set --fallback-level 3. This will make Sockseek launch additional searches (e.g. without the
        artist name) whenever it receives no results. Note that this can make searches slower and
        sometimes results in more incorrect files getting downloaded.
      Use --print results-full to inspect what Soulseek returned without downloading anything.

  Why does it rank MP3 above FLAC when I prefer FLAC?
    Preferences have an order: matching what you searched for comes before audio quality. With the
    default pref-strict-album, an MP3 album folder named after the album ranks above a FLAC folder
    that isn't. The same applies to song titles with pref-strict-title. See strict matching for what
    counts as a match.
    In the GUI, the info icon next to Other matches shows which preferences the other results miss.
    The ranking pills above the results show the preferences in their order.
    To get only FLAC, filter instead of ranking: use format = flac (--format flac), or select FLAC
    under Filtering in the GUI.

  CLI Searching
    - It's always best to provide the least input necessary to uniquely identify an album or song.
      - Sometimes including the artist can be undesirable (e.g. ""Various Artists""). For spotify or
        bandcamp inputs, you can remove the artist name with --regex A:.*.
      - Use --remove-ft to remove ""feat."" or ""ft."" artists
    - You can download an entire album based on the name of one of its songs by searching for that
      name in album mode: ""artist=ARTIST, title=SONG TITLE"" --album.
    - When searching for a single song with a string input, you can provide the album name in
      addition. The album name will not be included in the query, but search results containing it
      will be preferred (due to pref-strict-album).
    - When dealing with YouTube playlists you may want to remove any text in parentheses (like
      (Video)), as well as ""Official"" and ""Lyrics"" with --regex
      ""[\[\(].*?[\]\)]|(?i:lyrics)|(?i:official)""

  Testing Options
    You can test almost any aspect of the search and downloading logic with --mock-files-dir and
    pointing it to a local directory containing audio files. This directory will then be used
    instead of searching and downloading from Soulseek. Example:

    sockseek ""Artist - Album"" -t --mock-files-dir /path/to/dir

    If you plan to use a large music library, you might want to add --mock-files-no-read-tags to
    improve the initial loading performance. But note that reading tags is required when filtering
    by metadata such as length or bitrate.";

    const string daemonHelp = @"
Daemon and remote access
  The daemon serves the WebUI and the HTTP API. It listens on 127.0.0.1:5030 by default. To listen
  on another interface, set server-ip to its address. The Soulseek peer listener uses a separate
  listen-port.

  Start the daemon
    If you installed the release archive, run sockseek auth hash-password and enter the password you
    want to use for the WebUI. Add the printed hash to your sockseek.conf:

    web-password-hash = paste-the-hash-here

    Start the daemon on your computer:

    sockseek daemon --allow-http-auth

    Open http://127.0.0.1:5030 and sign in as admin with your WebUI password.

  Your first WebUI search or download
    Use the main search bar to inspect results and choose the files or folder you want. The search
    bar has several modes, which can be chosen with the button on the left. For an album, try Artist
    - Album Title. Group modes combine same songs or albums into groups so you can view an artist's
    discography in the search results. See more info about download modes.
    For an automatic download, create a download job in Searches → New Job. Sockseek can take a
    search string, a playlist, a CSV, or a list and pick matches using your file conditions. Filter
    conditions decide which results qualify. Ranking conditions help choose among the qualifying
    results. See download modes and supported inputs for examples.

  Share a folder
    Add a directory on the daemon machine to your config, for example share = /path/to/Music. Use
    System → Rescan shares to update the catalog, then check System → Shares to see what peers can
    browse. See the sharing and upload options.

  Ports and connectivity
    The daemon uses two ports:
    - 5030, the WebUI and API. It accepts connections from the same computer only. To reach it from
      other devices, set server-ip to the address to listen on, and put it behind HTTPS: sign-in
      over plain HTTP is refused unless you set allow-http-auth. See WebUI and access options.
    - 49998/TCP, the Soulseek peer listener (listen-port). Other Soulseek users connect to it to
      download from you and to send you files. Sockseek works without it being reachable, but
      transfers with peers who are also unreachable will fail. If you are behind a router, forward
      this port to the machine running the daemon, and allow it through that machine's firewall.

  Remote CLI
    Create a named API key in System → API. Copy the secret when it is shown and save it in a file
    on the client machine. You can now submit downloads from the CLI:

    sockseek ""Artist - Album Title"" --remote https://your-daemon --remote-api-key-file /path/to/key

    Use --detach to print the workflow ID and exit, or --monitor to watch active workflows. Files
    are downloaded to the daemon's output directory. The same connection options work with commands
    such as sockseek chat conversations and sockseek transfers. Put remote and remote-api-key-file
    in a configuration profile if you also use the CLI for local downloads.
    For key management, offline maintenance, and sharing options, see the daemon reference.";

    private static Dictionary<string, string> CommandTopics() => new(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
    {
        ["remote"] = @"
Remote CLI Options

  --remote <url>                  Connect to the daemon at this URL (required unless configured)
  --remote-api-key-file <path>    Read the API key from a file (preferred)
  --remote-api-key <key>          Supply the API key inline

  --json                          Print chat, room, notification, share and transfer results as JSON
  --limit <n>                     Maximum entries for chat, room, notification and transfer lists

  --detach                        Submit a download, print its workflow ID and exit
  --monitor                       Monitor all active workflows until interrupted
                                  Supply an input to submit it and monitor all daemon work",
        ["daemon"] = daemonHelp + "\n" + @"
Daemon Options

  sockseek daemon                 Start the HTTP/SignalR daemon
  --server-ip <ip>                IP/interface for the daemon HTTP API (default: 127.0.0.1)
  --server-port <port>            Port for the daemon HTTP API (default: 5030)
  --data-dir <path>               Directory for daemon data, including sockseek.db
  --no-config-watch               Disable automatic daemon config-file reload
  --no-docs                       Disable the bundled manual at /docs/
  --no-update-check               Do not ask GitHub daily whether a newer release exists

  WebUI and Access

    --web-username <name>           WebUI login name (default: admin)
    --web-password-file <path>      WebUI password file (required unless a hash or --no-auth is set)
    --web-password-hash <hash>      WebUI password hash generated by sockseek auth hash-password
    --web-session-lifetime <time>   Fixed session duration (default: 7.00:00:00)
    --trusted-proxies <ips>         Forwarding proxy IPs, comma-separated (default: 127.0.0.1,::1).
                                    Prefix with '+ ' to append one address.
    --allow-http-auth               Permit plaintext HTTP credentials/cookies (default: false)
    --no-auth                       Grant anonymous full access (default: false)

    --web-ui-dir <path>             Serve a custom WebUI from this directory (requires restart)
    --web-theme <theme>             WebUI theme: flat (default), soft, 2009
    --web-color-scheme <scheme>     WebUI color scheme: dark (default), light, auto (follow the browser)
    --web-default-search-mode <mode>
                                    generic, track, album (default), song-groups, album-groups
    --web-default-sort <sort>       Default sort for search results: relevance (default), upload-speed,
                                    queue-depth. Modes without that sort use relevance.

  Sharing, Uploads and Chat

    --share <path|[alias]path>      Share a local directory, optionally under an alias.
                                    The share root may be a symlink. Prefix with '+ ' to append.
    --share-exclude <path>          Exclude a local directory and its contents from shares.
                                    Prefix with '+ ' to append.
    --share-filter <regex>          Exclude files and directories whose remote relative paths
                                    match the pattern (case-insensitive). Prefix with '+ ' to append.
    --upload-blocked-user <names>   Block these users' access to your shares. Separate names with commas.
                                    Prefix with '+ ' to append one exact name.
    --upload-blocked-ip <addresses> Block access to your shares from these comma-separated IP addresses.
                                    Prefix with '+ ' to append one address.
    --private-message-blocked-user <names>
                                    Discard private messages from these users. Separate names with commas.
                                    Prefix with '+ ' to append one exact name.
    --chat-room <names>             Join these rooms on connect. Separate names with commas.
                                    Prefix with '+ ' to append one exact name.

    --share-search-result-limit <n> Max files sent per peer search reply (default: 500, 0 disables replies)
    --share-scan-on-start <bool>    Scan configured shares when the daemon starts (default: true)
    --share-rescan-interval <time>  Periodic rescan interval (for example 30m, 12h, or off;
                                    default: off)
    --upload-slots <num>            Maximum concurrent uploads (default: 10)
    --upload-speed-limit-kib <n>    Aggregate upload limit in KiB/s (default: unlimited)

  History Retention

    --no-retention                  Disable scheduled history retention
    --successful-job-retention-days <days|forever>
                                    Successful job retention (default: forever)
    --unsuccessful-job-retention-days <days|forever>
                                    Failed, cancelled, and interrupted job retention (default: forever)
    --transfer-retention-days <days|forever>
                                    Transfer history, attempts and byte accounting retention (default: forever)
    --search-result-retention-days <days|forever>
                                    Raw search-result retention (default: 30)
    --private-message-retention-days <days|forever>
                                    Private-message retention (default: forever)
    --room-message-retention-days <days|forever>
                                    Room-message retention (default: 30)",
        ["database"] = @"
Offline database maintenance

  sockseek database migrate      Update the offline database to the current schema
  sockseek database integrity    Check an offline database for corruption
  sockseek database backup       Create and verify an offline backup
  sockseek database restore      Verify and restore an offline backup
  --data-dir <path>               Override the configured/default data directory
  --backup <path>                 Backup destination for `backup`, source for `restore`",
        ["auth"] = @"
Authentication

  sockseek auth hash-password       Generate a WebUI password hash locally
    --web-password-file <path>      Read the password from a file (default: prompt or stdin)

  sockseek auth keys list           List API keys locally or on a running daemon
  sockseek auth keys create <name>  Create a named key and print its secret once
  sockseek auth keys revoke <id>    Revoke an API key
    --data-dir <path>               Key database directory for offline use (stop the daemon first)",
        ["user"] = @"
User Profiles

  sockseek user profile <username>  Show a peer's profile and sharing statistics
    --refresh                       Fetch new profile data
    --picture <mode>                Picture mode: auto|sixel|pixels|none; auto probes
                                    terminal support (default: auto)",
        ["chat"] = @"
Private messages

  sockseek chat status                Show chat status
  sockseek chat conversations [--unread]
                                      List private conversations
  sockseek chat messages <username>   Read a conversation
  sockseek chat send <username> <message>
                                      Send a private message
  sockseek chat read <username> [--through <message-id>]
                                      Mark a conversation as read
  sockseek chat delete <username>     Permanently delete a conversation and its messages",
        ["room"] = @"
Chat rooms

  sockseek room available|joined      List available or joined rooms
  sockseek room join <name> [--no-remember]
                                      Join a room
  sockseek room leave <name>          Leave a room
  sockseek room messages|members <name>
                                      List messages or members
  sockseek room send <name> <message> Send a room message
  sockseek room member add <name> <username>
                                      Add a private room member",
        ["notifications"] = @"
  sockseek notifications [--unread]   List notifications",
        ["notification"] = @"
  sockseek notification read <id|all> Mark one or all notifications as read",
        ["share"] = @"
Shares

  sockseek share status               Show the state of the share catalog
  sockseek share scan [--cancel]      Start or cancel a scan",
        ["transfers"] = @"
  sockseek transfers                  List transfers
    --direction upload|download       Filter by transfer direction
    --phase <phase>                   Filter by transfer phase
    --outcome <outcome>               Filter by terminal outcome
    --username <name>                 Filter by peer
    --include-removed                 Include transfers removed from history
    --cursor <cursor>                 Continue from an earlier page

  sockseek transfers cancel --direction upload|download
    --scope all|queued|in-progress    Select transfers to cancel (default: all)
  sockseek transfers remove           Remove matching transfers from history
    --direction upload|download       Filter by transfer direction
    --outcome <outcome>               Filter by terminal outcome
    --username <name>                 Filter either bulk action by peer
    --group-ref <ref>                 Limit either bulk action to a group (requires --username and --direction)",
        ["transfer"] = @"
  sockseek transfer cancel|retry|remove <id>
                                      Cancel one transfer or remove it from history
  sockseek job rerun <id>             Run a job again, reading its source (link, file path) as it is now
    --exact                           Run the items saved with the job instead of reading the source
  sockseek job retry-directory <id>   Download the files a folder job is still missing",
    };
}
